A||||12||batboy||steering column||||1
Z||000000||batboy||09-17-1999||11:03 PM||||The turn signal/cruise stalk is broken on my latest Fiero (85 GT). Also the left turn signal does not cancel and when you turn the steering wheel, there is a rubbing sound in the column. Tom (here at the Forum) has a column he will sell me, but recommends that it would be much cheaper to fix my existing column. He suggested that the bearings may be worn, hence the rubbing noise. I think I can manage replacing the cruise stalk, but have never been inside a column before. I do have a steering wheel puller but not a lock plate tool. I can buy or borrow one I suppose. In a past discussion, Ogre recommended just replacing the whole column rather than tearing into it. I'm looking for more opinions. How hard is it to fix the non-cancelling signal and to get at the bearings for an experienced shade tree mechanic. Would replacing the stalk possibly fix the cancelling problem? Intuition says no, I was thinking there is a concentric cam or something like that within the column. Any thoughts on the matter? Lastly, this is in a 4 speed car and it has a lever that you push to turn the ignition switch off that my other two auto Fieros don't have. If I was to replace the whole column, I suppose it has to be with a column out of another stick shift car, right? Suggestions? Comments? Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||09-18-1999||12:46 AM||||<BR>batboy, I say go for it and take the steering column apart. I did and there was nothing really to it. The biggest problem was removing the lock plate which requires a special tool. I'd be damned if I was going to buy a tool that I was only going to need one time, so I made one from a piece of flat iron and two bolts. Once you have the steering wheel off, you'll be able to see what it is you need to do to remove the rest.<P>I took mine apart because when I bought my Fiero, the steering wheel (and what it attaches to) was real sloppy in the steering column. It turned out that it was several bolts inside the column that were loose and this resulted in the whole inner assembly flopping around. When it was reassembled properly it worked like a charm. Nothing needed to be replaced.<P>A job like this should be a piece of cake for a fella with a V8 Fiero. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||14
Z||000002||ggarsk||09-18-1999||02:52 AM||||It all depends on what all is wrong with your present column. If you need a new turn signal unit thats around $35 plus if you need bearings and your time. The lock plate tool isn't that much and you will probally use it more then once. You'll have to take the lock plates off even if you get a new column to change your ignition switch. Changing a column is real simple...I just changed the one in my 84. I was lucky and got the new column from a junkyard for $25.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||09-18-1999||08:57 AM||||Thanks for the moral support guys. I have changed out columns before, that's no problem, it's just the fear of the unknown about taking one apart. I suppose I should just think of it as an adventure. Non-cruise columns are easy and cheap to find, but around here, ones with cruise and tilt are much harder to locate. I don't mind buying a lock plate tool, but if I do, am I going to be able to fix the "left turn signal doesn't cancel" problem? Are the new replacement bearings available anywhere? The cruise stalk is no problem, Tom has one he'll sell me for only $12. Thanks again.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-18-1999||12:20 PM||||2 things.... <P>if you only tightened 2 bolts that hold the tilt unit to the column shell, you missed 2. the other 2 can only be reached by splitting the tilt unit. Splitting the tilt unit is not a simple task, and can hurt you if you don't handle it with some care. If you didn't get all 4 then the unit can come loose again.<P>The turn signal cancel should be repairable. The part is usually in the red "Help" blister packs at many parts stores. Nearly any parts store should have it from either motoro mite, or someone else. If you are going this far, check the gears for the ignition switch and replace them if worn. At least regrease them. Since you need the stalk, and the cancel part at the least, as well as a lock plate tool, it might be cheaper to just replace teh whole column.<P>You can find replacement columns in many GM cars. They do not have to be Fiero. If you have an auto tranny use caution when removing the shift interlock cable. bag the end of the cable. If you bump the shifter youll likely knock the end apart. If the end is not bagged when that happens, the parts will go flying all over.||||reg||4
Z||000005||Patrick||09-18-1999||05:13 PM||||<BR>Ogre's post reminded me of what I did when I had my steering column apart a couple of years ago. Yes, it was the tilt mechanism that was all loose inside the column. I forget how many screws (bolts?) there were inside to tighten, but four sounds about right, and I know I got them all. I also used <B>Loctite</B> so I wouldn't have to do this again. <P>It's possible batboy, that if it's just your tilt mechanism that has worked it's way loose that your bearings are fine.<P>Now that I remember a bit more, the only problem I had was that I didn't quite set the linkage up properly inside the column which was supposed to engage the electrical contacts when the key was turned far enough to turn the starter. It worked properly at first, but of course, miles from home, on a stretch of road where I couldn't push start the car, turning the key would not engage the starter. Luckily I had a screwdriver in the car, so I reached into the engine compartment, shorted the appropriate terminals on the starter, and off I went. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Setting up the linkage properly was not difficult to do once I realized what I had done wrong.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000006||batboy||09-18-1999||06:48 PM||||Ogre and Patrick are scaring me. I don't know whether I should tear into this sucker or not. I'm not positive about the bearing thing. It doesn't seem to be loose, it just makes a little rubbing noise like something is binding inside the column when you turn the steering wheel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||09-18-1999||08:05 PM||||Is my face red or what, boy do I feel stupid. That rubbing noise was actually that, but not inside the column. Down past the U-joint where the steering shaft passes through the floor, it was rubbing against the rubber seal causing it to make that rubbing sound when I turned the wheel. A little WD-40 quieted it right down. The steer column feels solid, not much if any play in those dang bearings after all. Duh!<P>Of course I still have the broken cruise stalk and the left signal that won't cancel. I'll replace the signal/wiper/cruise stalk and after that, I might see about fixing the cancelling problem, but I'm feeling better about the situation now. Any tricks or hints I should know about before attempting to replace that stalk?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||09-18-1999||08:24 PM||||Don't even try to take the steering column apart without the proper tools. You'll need two pullys, one to get the steering wheel off and one to get the lock plate off. I don't think these tools are expensive.<P>The turn signal cancel is nothing more than a little spring. Most of the time these break off when you try to force the handle when your steering wheel is turned a little. That little broken spring can cause all kinds of nasty things inside the column. It can shorten the claxon (and you'll be driving with a continuous horn) but it can also lock up your steering wheel. Better get that piece out of there asap.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||09-18-1999||09:13 PM||||Thanks for the advice and warning Cliff. Of course, the Haynes manual is worthless in this case. I do have a wheel puller, but not the lock plate remover. I guess I might as well buy one to add to my tool collection. Who knows, if I get good enough at working on these Fieros, I might just take an early retirement in a few years and open up my own Fiero shop.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||09-18-1999||11:48 PM||||I had a relative with a body shop. My 3 main jobs:<P>1. fixing door machinary. I was the only one with the patience for it, and hands that were thin/flexible enough. (I still have healling cuts from the door I did, and was bitching about, last week.)<P>2. fixing steering columns. I was the only one that could handle the little bitty parts and not beat the hell out of it with a sledge.<P>3. under dash..... I was the only one that fit.<P>I've been doing this crap for over 20 years. When I tell you splitting the tilt unit can hurt you, I'm not doing it to blow air.... I'm glad you got off with a bit of spray on a noisy bushing.<P>the 2 specialty tools for steering column work will run you 30-40$US or more depending on where you shop. The turn signal part is a few more $, the stalk is a few more $. If you can get a whole working column for 50-60$us then it is well worth it. The column is held by only 5 bolts/nuts under the dash. ((Who cares about the keying..... Fiero doors and trunk use one key..... the ignition uses another.... As long as the replacement column comes with key it's a non issue.))<P>Granted with a replacement column you may still have to pull a wheel, but that takes about 15 minutes, and you can often borrow a puller for free plus a deposit. ||||reg||4
Z||000011||batboy||09-19-1999||10:59 AM||||Ogre, I hear you about working inside those doors. I just replaced an outer door handle and of course broke one of those plastic clips that hold the linkage on. After getting another clip and finally putting everything back together, I was nursing some small cuts and nicks to my hands and arms too. Did a bunch of cussing too, I'm sorry to say.<P>I will heed your warnings about the tilt column and if I do decide to tackle the job, I'll do my best to be careful. I've had cars fall off jacks on me and all sorts of diasters in the past and survived so far.<P>Trust me, I don't want to get maimed and I'm far more cautious now then in my young and stupid days. But, I'm not going to get scared off easily either. Heck, if I can put a 1940 Chevy Coupe body onto a Jeep 4X4 chassis, I'm hoping that I can handle a steering column. BTW, I do have a steering wheel puller, just not a lock plate tool. Thanks for the advice.||||reg||1
Z||000012||DJRice||09-20-1999||08:39 AM||||I bought a bearing kit for my 84 from a Chevy Dealership, and as I recall it was only about $10.  I took the column apart, then discovered how easy it was to switch entire colums and took the easy way out.<P>Thats funny about the cut up hands, we replaced a busted lock assembly in my brothers 86, and I have one skinless knuckle and several small cuts to show for it......||||reg||1
